uilding”). Alongside Proto-Germanic *twislaz, *twislō, whence the German doublet Zwiesel and Old English twisla (“bifurcation of a river”). The form Zwille, Zwele exists in a few High German dialects of the Low German borderland. It may have entered standard German thence or directly from Low German. In all these cases the zw- is due to secondary adaptation, perhaps by (etymologically correct) association with zwie-, zwei, Zweig.",{"g":"f","decl":"||||n|n|n|n"}]],"senses":[[574535,"zwille","noun","slingshot",0,"f"],[574536,"zwille","noun","alternative form of Zwiesel (“crotch of a tree”)",1,"f",["regional"]]]},"anbändelest":{"inflections":[["anbändelest","anbändeln","second-person,singular,subjunctive,subjunctive-i,subordinate-clause"]]},"anleiters":{"inflections":[["anleiters","anleiter","genitive"]]},"aufkönnten":{"inflections":[["aufkönnten","aufkönnen","first-person,plural,subjunctive,subjunctive-ii,subordinate-clause"]]},"bazillenträgers":{"inflections":[["bazillenträgers","bazillenträger","genitive"]]},"braunwurzen":{"inflections":[["braunwurzen","braunwurz","dative,definite,plural"]]},"flipprest":{"inflections":[["flipprest","flippern","second-person,singular,subjunctive,subjunctive-i"]]},"heranwachse":{"inflections":[["heranwachse","heranwachsen","first-person,indicative,present,singular,subordinate-clause"]]},"himbergs":{"inflections":[["himbergs","himberg","genitive"]]},"konsumzurückhaltungen":{"inflections":[["konsumzurückhaltungen","konsumzurückhaltung","plural"]]},"kürzerträte":{"inflections":[["kürzerträte","kürzertreten","first-person,formal,rare,singular,subjunctive,subjunctive-ii,subordinate-clause"]]},"nachgehangen":{"inflections":[["nachgehangen","nachhängen","participle,past"]]},"niederprüglest":{"inflections":[["niederprüglest","niederprügeln","second-person,singular,subjunctive,subjunctive-i,subordinate-clause"]]},"rekordstandes":{"inflections":[["rekordstandes","rekordstand","genitive"]]},"schirrtest":{"inflections":[["schirrtest","schirren","